          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The dominant traditional identification is the Church or people of God as a lesser, derived light reflecting Christ, while biblical usage remains context-dependent and includes judgment, sacred time, covenant permanence, and familial or Marian imagery.\",\"book_summary\":\"The moon signifies the faithful, reflective, and submissive member of a relationship, applied to the Son before the Father, the wife before her husband, and the Church before Christ.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Moon = the Church or people of God reflecting Christ's light.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Moon = the faithful, reflecting, submissive side of an ordered relationship.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains the familiar Church-reflecting-Christ case but generalizes it into a relational principle. It adds the Son reflecting and submitting to the Father and the wife reflecting and submitting to her husband, cases not included in the consensus's central lunar identification. It also makes submission, rather than reflected light alone, essential to the symbol.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction in the shared Church-Christ case. The material difference is that the book treats the Church reading as one instance of a broader hierarchy-and-reflection schema rather than as the moon's principal specific referent.\",\"rationale\":\"Because the book explicitly preserves the commonly taught Church-as-reflector identification, it does not replace the consensus core. It materially broadens the symbol's extension and changes its precision by identifying a generic relational role that can be instantiated by Christ, wives, and the Church. That is a substantive refinement rather than a mere wording difference or a wholly novel symbol.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Psalm 89:37 directly calls the moon a faithful witness, and the other passages establish Christ as faithful witness and describe submission or headship relationships. However, none explicitly identifies the moon with the Son, a wife, or the generic submissive party, nor do they establish that lunar reflection signifies submission. The broader equation therefore depends on an analogical synthesis rather than direct textual identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
